      "writtenOn": "2024-11-08T08:07:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I thought that the local part is actually case sensitive:\nhttps://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5321#:~:text\u003dThe%0A%20%20%20local%2Dpart%20of%20a%20mailbox%20MUST%20BE%20treated%20as%20case%20sensitive.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "58615f27_9cfe4a22",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-08T09:38:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You are actually right, but then Gerrit misbehave anyway, because users are NOT allowed to have two emails with different local part cases.\n\nExample:\n1. Create an empty Gerrit with admin having email admin@mycompany.com\n2. Try to add a secondary email having ADMIN@mycompany.com\n\nGerrit *must* allow that, isn\u0027t it? Instead we get:\n```\nAn error occurred\nError 409 (Conflict): email exists\n\nEndpoint: /account/self/emails/*\n```",
      "parentUuid": "ee8213f2_4e2b0343",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "41812744_b0c61df2",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-11T07:45:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This may be a bug, but I guess it depends on whether all email providers support case sensitive local parts. If one email provider treats local parts as case-insensitive allowing this would open the door for duplicate emails.",
      "parentUuid": "58615f27_9cfe4a22",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "cf1fe38c_5f099061",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-11T07:47:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This may be a bug, but I guess it depends on whether all email providers support case sensitive local parts. If one email provider treats local parts as case-insensitive allowing this would open the door for duplicate emails.\n\nExactly, that\u0027s why I was thinking about a configuration similarly to what we do with usernames. Typically in companies the username is also used in the local part of the e-mail, and if the username is case insensitive then also the local part is treated as case-insensitive.",
      "parentUuid": "41812744_b0c61df2",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "732b3267_5d0d9561",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003935
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-11T07:57:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "But doing this is only safe if all email providers treat the local part as case-insensitive. If there is one email provider that treats the local part as case-sensitive, then I can take over your email, by registering the same email with another casing at that email provider and then make look like I\u0027m you.\n\nThe difference with usernames is that you as company have full control over this, with email providers this is different.",
      "parentUuid": "cf1fe38c_5f099061",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "721482b3_a7e14314",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1006192
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-11T08:50:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e But doing this is only safe if all email providers treat the local part as case-insensitive. \n\nWell, not if that\u0027s configurable with a `emailCaseSensitive` switch. I am sure the Gerrit admin knows if the e-mail provider is case insensitive or not on the local part of the e-mail.\n\n\u003e If there is one email provider that treats the local part as case-sensitive, then I can take over your email, by registering the same email with another casing at that email provider and then make look like I\u0027m you.\n\nSure, that\u0027s why we must keep the current behaviour by default, but making it consistent. At the moment is highly inconsistent: we treat them as case-sensitive or case-insensitive depending on the API, which isn\u0027t good IMHO.\n\n\u003e The difference with usernames is that you as company have full control over this, with email providers this is different.\n\nWell, sort of: the company knows which e-mail provider is using and therefore can configure the flag on `gerrit.config` accordingly.",
      "parentUuid": "732b3267_5d0d9561",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "95d113dd_ab90a16b",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002666
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-12T22:42:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Well, sort of: the company knows which e-mail provider is using and therefore can configure the flag on `gerrit.config` accordingly.\n\nI would think that kind of configuration would be per-domain then? i.e. be safe and assume email providers are case sensitive, but allow admins to set something like `caseInsensitiveLocalPart \u003d example.com` to indicate that a given domain has a case insensitive local part.\n\nI think you\u0027d still need to post-process query results if you run a case insensitive search using only the local part in order to check if the domain is in the configured list.",
      "parentUuid": "721482b3_a7e14314",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 38
      },
      "revId": "28cf0ac46588255d161818f7c10d099468bb67a4",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
